### Generic Classes and Methods in Production Architecture

In enterprise Java applications, generic classes and methods are fundamental to building reusable infrastructure such as:
- **Data Repositories:** `CrudRepository<T, ID>`
- **Cache Managers:** `Cache<K, V>`
- **API Response Wrappers:** `ApiResponse<T>`

### Building an Enterprise `ApiResponse<T>` Wrapper

```java
public class ApiResponse<T> {
    private final boolean success;
    private final String message;
    private final T data;

    public ApiResponse(boolean success, String message, T data) {
        this.success = success;
        this.message = message;
        this.data = data;
    }

    public static <T> ApiResponse<T> success(T data) {
        return new ApiResponse<>(true, "Operation successful", data);
    }

    public static <T> ApiResponse<T> error(String message) {
        return new ApiResponse<>(false, message, null);
    }

    public boolean isSuccess() { return success; }
    public String message() { return message; }
    public T getData() { return data; }
}
```

### Client Usage
```java
ApiResponse<User> userRes = ApiResponse.success(new User("ashok"));
ApiResponse<List<Order>> ordersRes = ApiResponse.success(orderList);
```
Both endpoints share the exact same JSON envelope format while guaranteeing compile-time type safety for the `data` payload!

### API Anatomy: Bounded Wildcards in Enterprise APIs

When designing library APIs or shared services, methods that accept generic collections must be flexible enough to accommodate subtypes and supertypes.

### The PECS Rule in Practice

```java
public class CollectionUtils {
    // Producer: We only read Numbers from 'source' -> Use <? extends Number>
    public static double sum(List<? extends Number> source) {
        double total = 0.0;
        for (Number num : source) {
            total += num.doubleValue();
        }
        return total;
    }

    // Consumer: We only write Integers into 'destination' -> Use <? super Integer>
    public static void addNumbers(List<? super Integer> destination) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            destination.add(i); // Valid! destination accepts Integer
        }
    }
}
```

### Why This Flexibility Matters:
- `sum(List<Integer>)`, `sum(List<Double>)`, and `sum(List<Float>)` can all be passed to `sum()` because `Integer`, `Double`, and `Float` extend `Number`.
- `addNumbers(List<Integer>)`, `addNumbers(List<Number>)`, and `addNumbers(List<Object>)` can all be passed to `addNumbers()` because `Integer`, `Number`, and `Object` are supertypes of `Integer`.

### Worked Examples: Generic In-Memory Data Store

Let's build a functional, generic in-memory repository supporting CRUD operations:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.Predicate;

public class InMemoryRepository<T, ID> {
    private final Map<ID, T> storage = new HashMap<>();

    public void save(ID id, T entity) {
        if (id == null || entity == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("ID and entity must not be null");
        }
        storage.put(id, entity);
    }

    public Optional<T> findById(ID id) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(storage.get(id));
    }

    public List<T> findAll(Predicate<T> filter) {
        List<T> matches = new ArrayList<>();
        for (T item : storage.values()) {
            if (filter.test(item)) {
                matches.add(item);
            }
        }
        return matches;
    }

    public long count() {
        return storage.size();
    }
}
```

### Testing the Repository
```java
InMemoryRepository<String, Long> userRepo = new InMemoryRepository<>();
userRepo.save(1L, "Alice");
userRepo.save(2L, "Bob");

Optional<String> user = userRepo.findById(1L);
user.ifPresent(System.out::println); // Prints: Alice
```

### Debugging and Failure Analysis: Generic Type Erasure & Bridge Methods

When troubleshooting advanced generic code, understanding **Bridge Methods** and **Heap Pollution** is essential.

### 1. Bridge Methods
Consider extending a generic class and overriding a method:

```java
public class Node<T> {
    public void setData(T data) { ... }
}

public class MyStringNode extends Node<String> {
    @Override
    public void setData(String data) { ... }
}
```

After type erasure, `Node.setData` has parameter type `Object`.
To preserve polymorphism (so that calling `((Node) myStringNode).setData(obj)` invokes the overridden method), the compiler synthesizes a hidden **Bridge Method**:

```java
// Synthetic compiler-generated bridge method in MyStringNode:
public void setData(Object data) {
    setData((String) data); // Casts and delegates to typed method!
}
```

---

### 2. Heap Pollution
Heap pollution occurs when a variable of a parameterized type refers to an object that is not of that parameterized type, typically caused by raw type casting:

```java
List<String> strings = new ArrayList<>();
List raw = strings; // Raw type warning!
raw.add(12345);     // Heap pollution: Integer added to List<String>

// Later in code:
String s = strings.get(0); // ClassCastException: Integer cannot be cast to String!
```

> [!WARNING]
> Never mix raw types with parameterized types. Always address compiler warnings about unchecked operations.

